data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing modifies an organism's genome by delivering the Cas9 nuclease with a synthetic guide RNA, which cuts the genome at a desired location [1].\n- It is a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral defense system [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable \"molecular scissors\" that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In bacteria and archaea, CRISPR sequences derive from bacteriophage DNA and are used to detect and destroy similar phages during later infections [2].\n- Headlines report CRISPR applications in disease, including a 2021 claim about treating a broad array of diseases [4] and a 2024 report that HIV was eliminated in cell culture [5]."}

